/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Kant Lab shared glossary — German (and Latin) terms with ONE fixed English rendering
   HAND-CURATED. Edit this file directly; every translation run reads it.

   Purpose: every translation of the corpus (Meier, Baumgarten, the Logik transcripts, the
   Reflexionen) renders a term the same way, and the reader can always show which German
   word stood behind an English one. The termbase is the scholarly asset; translations are
   regenerated from it. See ../translation-plan.html.

   STATUS: v0.1, PROPOSED. Every entry is a proposal for a person who reads Kant in German
   to confirm, with the Cambridge Edition glossaries at hand (Young, Lectures on Logic;
   Guyer & Wood, Critique of Pure Reason; Fugate & Hymers, Baumgarten's Metaphysics). When
   an entry is confirmed, set status: 'confirmed' and say who and when in `by`.

   Entry fields
     id      stable identifier, lower case, never reused
     de      the lemma in modern spelling (what you look it up under)
     v       period spellings and odd forms the transcripts use, as printed. The matcher
             also normalises ß/ss, th/t, ey/ei, zz/tz, -zion/-tion, c/k, doubled consonants
             and a lengthening h on its own, so list only what those rules miss
     f       inflected forms the matcher cannot derive by adding an ending (umlaut plurals:
             Grund → Gründe)
     pos     n noun · v verb · a adjective/adverb · p phrase/idiom · l Latin
     en      THE English rendering. Translations must use it unless a sense below applies
     senses  [{ en, when }]: other renderings, each with the condition that selects it.
             The first condition that fits wins; otherwise `en`
     la      Latin equivalents (Meier and Baumgarten gloss themselves in Latin)
     avoid   renderings that are wrong here, typically the familiar false friend
     match   for checking a translation: lower-case stems any of which counts as the
             rendering being present (defaults to `en`)
     d       domain: logic · cognition · mind · metaphysics · practical · aesthetics ·
             scholarship · idiom
     b       basis: convention (the usual rendering in English Kant scholarship) · meier
             (Meier's own terminology, fixed by his Latin glosses) · proposal (a choice
             made here that a person should confirm)
     note    anything a translator or reader needs to know
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

